      "claim": "In humans, the tocotrienol-rich fraction of palm oil has poor oral bioavailability; tocotrienols share a polar chromanol head and a long isoprenoid side chain with the query ubiquinone, supporting low F for this highly lipophilic isoprenoid.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C17"],
      "claim": "In humans, a deuterated lycopene study reported a lycopene bioavailability estimate of only 1.2%, indicating very poor systemic availability for a long polyisoprenoid lipid similar in lipophilicity to the query.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C07"],
      "claim": "In humans, only 0.17% of a 23.6 mg lycopene dose from tomato paste reached the circulation, reinforcing the poor oral absorption of long-chain isoprenoid lipids.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C02"],
      "claim": "In humans, phytoene absolute bioavailability was 58.5 ± 18.9%, showing that some acyclic carotenoids can be well absorbed; this is contradictory high evidence, but transferability to the quinone-containing CoQ10 is limited.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C13"],
      "claim": "In a 13C-lycopene study, all-trans lycopene bioavailability was 23.2 ± 8%, above the 20% threshold; this is contradictory high evidence for a carotenoid, but other lycopene records and the query's quinone/isoprenoid structure make it less decisive.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "The query is ubiquinone-10 (CoQ10), a high-molecular-weight, highly lipophilic polyisoprenylated benzoquinone. The most transferable human evidence—poor tocotrienol bioavailability and very low lycopene systemic availability—supports F < 20%. Occasional high carotenoid values are less transferable and do not overcome the consistent low-absorption pattern for long-chain isoprenoid lipids. Therefore predict low.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct CoQ10/ubiquinone absolute oral bioavailability record is present in the supplied evidence.",
    "Carotenoid data are conflicting (phytoene and labeled lycopene high vs conventional lycopene low), and their relevance to a quinone-headed isoprenoid is uncertain.",
    "Formulation, food matrix, and dose form, which strongly affect lipophilic isoprenoid absorption, are not matched to the query."
